$DIR/main.p:4:22: cannot store c in package variable kept, which outlives c
    c (declared at main.p:4) is a chan int, a counted reference to a channel: an
    ordinary interface that lends the channel does not count a reference of its
    own, so it may not outlive c, whose reference is dropped at the end of its
    scope in f. When f returns, c drops its reference, and the channel is freed
    if that was the last one, but package variables live for the whole program,
    so package variable kept would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store c itself, changing the type of kept to chan int: it then counts a
    reference of its own:
        kept = c
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
